Description: pvr: cache compiled end-of-tile and load op shaders
 pvr_sub_cmd_gfx_per_job_fragment_programs_create_and_upload() builds the
 end-of-tile USC program of every graphics job with pvr_usc_eot(), a full
 NIR and PCO compile (pco_preprocess_nir() to pco_encode_ir()), and then
 uploads the binary into the command buffer. Under zink every render pass is
 a graphics job, so every render pass costs one such compile on the
 recording thread. The
 program depends only on the end-of-tile properties: emit count, the PBE
 state words, the layer strides, the tile buffer addresses, the sample count
 and the output register count; they repeat for the same render targets.
 .
 With dynamic rendering every vkCmdBeginRendering that loads or clears a
 colour attachment also builds its load op programs:
 pvr_arch_mrt_load_ops_setup() calls pvr_mrt_load_op_init() and
 pvr_arch_load_op_shader_generate(), which compiles the USC shader with
 pvr_uscgen_loadop(), again a full NIR and PCO compile, before uploading it
 with its PDS programs. Render pass objects do this once in
 vkCreateRenderPass, zink pays it for every render pass on the recording
 thread.
 .
 Keep the compiled binaries in two hash tables per device under one lock.
 The end-of-tile table is keyed by the end-of-tile properties and keeps the
 temp register count with the binary. The load op table is keyed by what
 pvr_uscgen_loadop() reads: clear, load, 3D-view and unresolved MSAA masks,
 the RTA load flag, the destination formats of the loaded or cleared render
 targets, their MRT resources (type, intermediate size, output register or
 tile buffer and offset) and the depth clear register. A load op entry also
 keeps what the PDS fragment program and the load op state take from the
 shader: temp count, entry offset, phase change, shared register counts and
 tile buffer count. The command buffer and each load op still upload their
 own copy of the binary and their PDS programs, so ownership and lifetime of
 the device memory do not change and nothing in the caches is used by the
 GPU. The other users of pvr_usc_eot() (transfer context, SPM end-of-tile
 state) are not changed.
 .
 Each table holds up to 256 entries. Load op entries are immutable and stay
 until the device is destroyed; once the table is full, new load op shaders
 are compiled as before and not kept. The end-of-tile key contains the PBE
 state words, which hold the address of each render target, and the tile
 buffer addresses, so a workload that renders to many distinct surfaces,
 for example a compositor that allocates a new buffer for each window and
 frame, keeps adding entries that are not used again. The shader cannot be
 made independent of the addresses by loading the state words from shared
 registers: the PDS code segment of the render job is a single static
 program that only starts the USC task, the PDS data segment holds no DMA or
 register writes and the shared register size of the job is fixed; only the
 transfer context uses that mode. End-of-tile entries are therefore kept on
 a list in order of use and count their users: pvr_eot_cache_get() takes a
 reference and pvr_eot_cache_entry_release() drops it. When the table is
 full, the least recently used entry without users is destroyed to make
 room. An entry that is not in the table, because every entry is in use or
 because it could not be inserted, is freed on its last release.
 .
 Only for this downstream stack; not intended for upstream submission.
